// The three outcomes a test can report from inside the binary: a pass, its own failed assertion,
// and a foreign error that reached the end of the body unhandled.
//
// Assertions are written with `Expect` (stdlib/Testing.maxon) rather than a hand-rolled
// `throw TestFailure.assertion`, and that is the whole difference between a report a reader can
// act on and one they cannot. Both fail the test; only `Expect` still has BOTH VALUES in hand at
// the assertion site, so only `Expect` can say `expected: 5` / `received: 4`. A caught error in
// Maxon is an enum with no payload, so a failure that does not print at the site can never be
// reconstructed afterwards — the runner would have nothing to print but the headline.
//
// `missingUser` is deliberately NOT exported. It is a fixture private to this file, and the
// generated dispatcher is appended to this file precisely so that neither it nor the test symbols
// it calls have to be exported to be reachable.

function missingUser() returns String
	return "nobody"
end 'missingUser'

test 'adds two numbers'
	try Expect.equal(add(2, b: 2), expected: 4)
end 'adds two numbers'

test 'returns 404 when the user is missing'
	let found = try lookup(missingUser())
	try Expect.equal(found, expected: 1)
end 'returns 404 when the user is missing'

test 'fails its own assertion'
	try Expect.equal(add(2, b: 2), expected: 5)
end 'fails its own assertion'
